"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[97695],{97695:e=>{e.exports="# Database Connector Diagnostics\n\nDatabase connectors support two extraction modes and multiple network connectivity options. Establish which combination is in use before diagnosing — the required permissions, prerequisites, and failure modes differ significantly.\n\n## Identifying the Active Configuration\n\nThe pipeline spec is available from agent context, available pipeline tools, or `GET /api/2.0/pipelines/<pipeline-id>`. Inspect these fields:\n\n**Step 1 — Check `pipeline_type`**\n\n| Value                 | Meaning                                                                                                                                                                                                                    |\n| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `\"INGESTION_GATEWAY\"` | This spec describes a **gateway pipeline**. It extracts CDC data but does not load it. The paired ingestion pipeline (which references this gateway's ID) does the loading. Diagnose the ingestion pipeline, not this one. |\n| `\"MANAGED_INGESTION\"` | This is the data-loading pipeline. Continue to Step 2.                                                                                                                                                                     |\n\n**Step 2 — For `MANAGED_INGESTION`, check `ingestion_definition`**\n\n| Field present          | Value                                               | Mode                                                                                                                                                           |\n| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `ingestion_gateway_id` | any non-null value                                  | **CDC — Gateway architecture**: CDC data flows through a separate gateway pipeline. The gateway ID points to the paired `INGESTION_GATEWAY` pipeline.          |\n| `connection_name`      | set, and `connector_type` = `\"CDC\"`                 | **CDC — Direct (integrated connector)**: A single pipeline reads change data directly from the source using the Unity Catalog connection. No separate gateway. |\n| `connection_name`      | set, and `connector_type` absent or `\"QUERY_BASED\"` | **Query-based (QBC)**: Reads data via SQL SELECT. No CDC prerequisites needed.                                                                                 |\n\nOnce the mode is confirmed, use the relevant sections below.\n\n## Extraction Modes\n\n### Query-based (QBC)\n\nQuery-based connectors read data via SQL. Required permissions are simpler: SELECT on the tables being ingested is sufficient. No CDC prerequisites (binlog, WAL, supplemental logging) are needed. Query-based pipelines can run on serverless or classic compute.\n\n### CDC mode\n\nCDC connectors read change data from the database's replication log. This requires additional permissions and source configuration beyond SELECT — see [CDC Permissions](#cdc-permissions) and [CDC Source Prerequisites](#cdc-source-prerequisites) below.\n\nCDC mode also requires choosing a pipeline architecture — see [CDC Pipeline Architecture](#cdc-pipeline-architecture) below.\n\n## Network Connectivity\n\nHow the pipeline reaches the source database depends on the compute type and workspace configuration. Confirm which path is in use before diagnosing connectivity failures.\n\n> **Note on connector tools**: Connector-specific diagnostic tools make direct connections to the source database from serverless compute. They are only available when the workspace has a network path from serverless to the database — either a publicly accessible database endpoint, PNG, or PrivateLink. If none of these are configured, these tools will not be able to reach the database; fall back to asking the user to run diagnostics from their own environment instead.\n\n### Classic compute with gateway\n\nA Lakeflow Connect gateway runs on classic compute in the customer's environment and acts as a network bridge to the source. The gateway must be running and able to reach the database host on the database port. Verify gateway status first — a stopped or misconfigured gateway surfaces as a generic connection error that looks like an auth or network issue.\n\n### Serverless with PrivateLink\n\nWhen PNG is not enabled, serverless pipelines require Databricks PrivateLink to be configured between the workspace and the source database. If the user is on serverless compute and connectivity is failing, confirm PrivateLink is configured before looking at credentials or permissions.\n\n### Private Network Gateway (PNG)\n\nWhen PNG is enabled for the account, serverless pipelines can reach private databases through the managed Private Network Gateway without requiring a customer-deployed classic compute gateway. Verify that PNG is enabled for the workspace and that the source database's network allows inbound connections from the PNG.\n\n## CDC Pipeline Architecture\n\nFor CDC mode, the pipeline can be configured in one of two ways:\n\n### Integrated CDC connector\n\nA single managed pipeline reads change data directly from the source using the Unity Catalog connection. No separate ingestion gateway is provisioned. This is the simpler setup; use this when the network path supports it.\n\n### Gateway + ingestion pipeline\n\nA separate ingestion gateway is provisioned to extract change data from the source, and a separate ingestion pipeline loads it into the destination. This requires the gateway to be running, healthy, and reachable from the source database. Use this when the integrated connector is not available for the connector type.\n\nIf the user switched between these two modes after the pipeline was created, the pipeline cannot be modified in place — it must be recreated.\n\n## CDC Permissions\n\nCDC requires more than SELECT. Verify the service account has the CDC-specific permissions for the database:\n\n| Database   | Required permissions                                                                                                           | Docs                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| MySQL      | `REPLICATION SLAVE`, `REPLICATION CLIENT` on `*.*`; `SELECT(plugin,user)` on `mysql.user`; `SELECT` on target tables           | [MySQL privileges](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/mysql-privileges)                                                                                |\n| PostgreSQL | `REPLICATION` privilege (standard) or `rds_replication` role (AWS RDS), plus `CONNECT`, `USAGE` on schemas, `SELECT` on tables | [PostgreSQL privileges](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/postgresql-privileges)                                                                      |\n| SQL Server | `VIEW DATABASE PERFORMANCE STATE`, `SELECT` on system objects and target schemas                                               | [SQL Server privileges](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/sql-server-privileges)                                                                      |\n| Oracle     | `LOGMINING` system privilege, `EXECUTE_CATALOG_ROLE` role, `SELECT` on target tables                                           | [Oracle LogMiner required privileges](https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/19/sutil/oracle-logminer-utility.html#GUID-E6E58E55-B2D2-4C6B-8A33-2B4B25DE0B1A) |\n\n## CDC Source Prerequisites\n\nChange tracking must be enabled on the source database — it is **off by default** on most managed database services. **When these prerequisites are missing, the pipeline fails with `INGESTION_GATEWAY_CDC_NOT_ENABLED`.** Always mention this error code when helping a user diagnose CDC prerequisite failures.\n\n| Database   | Prerequisite                                                                                      |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| MySQL      | Binary logging enabled with `binlog_format=ROW` and `binlog_row_image=FULL`                       |\n| PostgreSQL | `wal_level=logical`; a logical replication slot must exist (`pg_create_logical_replication_slot`) |\n| Oracle     | Supplemental logging enabled (`ALTER DATABASE ADD SUPPLEMENTAL LOG DATA`)                         |\n| SQL Server | CDC enabled per-table (`sys.sp_cdc_enable_table`)                                                 |\n\nIf the user recently migrated to a managed database service (RDS, CloudSQL, Azure DB), these settings are often reset and need to be re-enabled.\n\n### Verifying CDC is enabled (`INGESTION_GATEWAY_CDC_NOT_ENABLED`)\n\nThe `INGESTION_GATEWAY_CDC_NOT_ENABLED` error means CDC prerequisites are not active on the source database. Confirm which prerequisites are missing using these queries:\n\n| Database   | Verification                                                                                                                                    |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| MySQL      | `SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'log_bin'; SHOW VARIABLES LIKE 'binlog_format';` — expect `ON` and `ROW`                                                   |\n| PostgreSQL | `SHOW wal_level;` — expect `logical`                                                                                                            |\n| Oracle     | `SELECT log_mode FROM v$database;` (expect `ARCHIVELOG`) and `SELECT supplemental_log_data_min FROM v$database;` (expect `YES`)                 |\n| SQL Server | `SELECT name, is_cdc_enabled FROM sys.databases;` (database level) and `SELECT name, is_tracked_by_cdc FROM sys.tables;` (per-table CDC status) |\n\nFor SQL Server Change Tracking specifically: `SELECT DB_NAME(database_id), is_change_tracking_on FROM sys.change_tracking_databases;`\n\nIf not enabled, enable CDC or Change Tracking on the affected tables and restart the pipeline to resolve `INGESTION_GATEWAY_CDC_NOT_ENABLED`.\n\n## Common Issues\n\n### Schema Changes (DDL)\n\nAdding or removing columns, or changing column types on source tables, can break a running CDC pipeline. If a pipeline stopped working after a recent schema change, the affected tables likely need to be resynced from scratch.\n\n## Monitoring Gateway Pipeline Progress\n\nFor pipelines using the **Gateway + ingestion pipeline** architecture, the gateway emits `flow_progress` events approximately every 5 minutes. These provide per-table row counts for both the initial snapshot and ongoing CDC phases.\n\n> **Which pipeline ID to use**: Always query `event_log()` with the **gateway pipeline's** ID — the `INGESTION_GATEWAY` pipeline that extracts data from the source. Passing the ingestion pipeline ID to `event_log()` will return no `flow_progress` results.\n\nQuery the gateway pipeline's event log:\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  origin.dataset_name,\n  origin.flow_name,\n  details:flow_progress:metrics:num_upserted_rows::bigint AS num_upserted_rows,\n  COALESCE(details:flow_progress:metrics:num_deleted_rows::bigint, 0) AS num_deleted_rows,\n  timestamp\nFROM event_log('<gateway_pipeline_id>')\nWHERE event_type = 'flow_progress'\n  AND level = 'METRICS'\n  AND origin.pipeline_type = 'INGESTION_GATEWAY'\n  AND timestamp > current_timestamp() - INTERVAL 1 DAY\nORDER BY timestamp DESC\nLIMIT 100\n```\n\nThe `flow_name` field indicates the replication phase:\n\n- `{catalog}.{schema}.{table}_snapshot_flow` — initial full-table copy; `num_upserted_rows` should increase between events\n- `{catalog}.{schema}.{table}_cdc_flow` — incremental CDC; zero upserts is normal when no source changes have occurred\n\n### Diagnosing stalled pipelines\n\n- **No events appearing**: Wait at least 5 minutes after pipeline start. If no events appear after that, verify the pipeline is running and that `pipelines.gateway.progressEventsEnabled` is `true` in the pipeline configuration (default for new pipelines).\n- **Snapshot stalled**: Zero `num_upserted_rows` across multiple consecutive events during the snapshot phase indicates a stall — investigate gateway logs.\n- **CDC appears stalled**: Absence of events entirely is the signal; zero-valued metrics during CDC are expected when the source has no changes.\n- **Metrics reset to zero after restart**: Expected behavior — metrics are in-memory and reset on pipeline restart, refresh, or resume.\n\nTo adjust emission frequency, set `pipelines.gateway.progressEventEmitFrequencySeconds` in the pipeline configuration (default 300; range 30–3600).\n"}}]);
